.TH PPK 1 2026-10-20 "Peios ppk 0.1.0"
.SH NAME
ppk \- create and manage Peios packages
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ppk pack
.RB [ \-\-artifact\-dir
.IR dir ]
.I package path
.br
.B ppk help
.RI [ command ]
.br
.B ppk
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help | \-V | \-\-version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ppk
creates Peios packages and stores them in an artifact directory.
.PP
A package is a manifest that lists every path in it, with each file's
content stored separately as a blob named by its SHA-256 hash.
The package's identity is the SHA-256 hash of its manifest, written as
.B sha256:
followed by the hash in lowercase hexadecimal.
.PP
A
.I package
argument is an identifier of the form
.IB name @ version\c
.RB [ ?arch= \c
.IR arch ].
Each of
.IR name ,
.IR version ,
and
.I arch
consists of ASCII letters, digits, and the characters
.BR . ,
.BR _ ,
.BR + ,
.BR \- ,
and
.BR ~ ,
and starts with a letter or digit.
Without
.BR ?arch= ,
the architecture is the one
.B ppk
was built for.
Use
.B noarch
for content that does not depend on the architecture.
No other qualifier is accepted, and
.B arch
can be given only once.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI pack " package path"
Pack the directory
.I path
as
.I package
and print the package's identity on standard output.
.IP
Regular files, symbolic links, and empty directories are recorded.
Directories with contents are implied by them.
A symbolic link is recorded with its target exactly as it reads and is never followed.
Of a file's permissions, only whether any execute bit is set is recorded.
.IP
.B ppk pack
refuses a directory that holds anything else, such as a socket or a device;
a file with its set-user-ID or set-group-ID bit set;
or a path or link target that is not valid UTF-8.
It also refuses an artifact directory inside
.IR path .
.IP
If
.I package
is already packed with the same contents,
.B ppk pack
succeeds without changing anything.
If it is packed with different contents,
.B ppk pack
fails and writes nothing: a packed version is never replaced.
.TP
.BI help " \fR[\fPcommand\fR]\fP"
Print help for
.B ppk
or for one of its commands.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-artifact\-dir " dir"
Store packages in
.I dir
instead of
.IR /system/artifacts .
.B ppk
creates the directory if it does not exist.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the program name and version and exit.
.SH SECURITY
.B ppk
makes no access checks of its own.
It needs write access to the artifact directory and read access to the
directory being packed.
Every file it writes in the artifact directory is read-only.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including printing help or the version.
.TP
.B 1
Failure, such as an unreadable file, an unwritable artifact directory, or a
version that is already packed with different contents.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error: an unknown option, a missing argument, or a malformed
.IR package .
.SH FILES
Paths are relative to the artifact directory,
.I /system/artifacts
by default.
.TP
.IR blobs/sha256/ hash
The content of one file, uncompressed, named by the SHA-256 hash of that content.
.TP
.IR manifests/sha256/ hash
A manifest, named by the SHA-256 hash of its bytes.
.TP
.IR refs/ name / version / arch
The identity of the package with that name, version, and architecture,
followed by a newline.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B ppk pack 'dev.peios.hello@1.0?arch=noarch' hello \-\-artifact\-dir artifacts
Pack the directory
.I hello
into
.I artifacts
and print the package's identity.
The identifier is quoted because some shells treat
.B ?
as a wildcard.
.SH SEE ALSO
https://learn.peios.org/peios/developing-for-peios/packaging/creating-a-package
